  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday night through next Monday)\nIssued at 1007 PM CDT Mon Aug 10 2026\n\nAs the aforementioned trough out west, associated with a nearly \nclosed low across SRN BC/NRN WA, edges eastward, flow becomes \nincreasingly southwesterly.  This transition, occurring during the \nearly morning hours of Thursday, will bring a more favorable pattern \nfor precipitation.  A tropical wave, which had been suggested the \npast few evenings, looks to no longer be at play for mid-week though \nthere could be increasing Gulf moisture in the mid levels by another \npotential wave this coming weekend. Still, it's models trying to \nmanage a weak feature and this is something they do not typically\nhandle well. The best opportunity for storms appears to be \nThursday evening and again on Friday though perhaps favoring the \nfar northwestern zones on the latter. Present indications suggest\nwe'll be back to dry for the weekend as the trough ejects into \nthe northern plains thus allowing the ridge to become firmly \nentrenched across the southern CONUS once again. Temperatures on \nThu/Fri look maybe 3-4 degrees cooler areawide with the extra \nclouds and lower geopotential heights. Have no fear, things should\nrebound to what we've experienced the past few days by next \nMonday.",
